---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// "Truthiness portability" — the guide to analyse mode (ADR-022 Part B). A
// FlatBars template renders under ONE engine's truthiness rule, but the same
// template may branch differently on another (Handlebars vs Mustache vs …).
// Analyse mode renders against real data and reports every condition whose branch
// would flip elsewhere, with a portable fix. This is NOT a fifth surface; it is a
// lint over a real render. Every example comes from the one gate-validated source
// (../analyse.mjs, gated by scripts/check-tutorial-tooling.mjs via the engine's
// `analyze`), so the page can never claim a finding the analyser does not produce.
// The normative text lives in docs/ (adr-0022-analyse-mode.adoc).
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import AnalyseGallery from "../components/AnalyseGallery.jsx";
import { examples as ex, gallery } from "../analyse.mjs";

const t = (s) => s; // readability helper for inline tag literals

const groups = [
  {
    label: "Truthiness",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "Why truthiness travels badly" },
      { href: "#empty-string", label: "Empty string" },
      { href: "#zero", label: "Zero" },
      { href: "#collections", label: "Empty array & object" },
      { href: "#portable", label: "What's already portable" },
      { href: "#potential", label: "Observed vs potential" },
      { href: "#suppress", label: "Suppressing with a schema" },
      { href: "#gallery", label: "Portability at scale" },
      { href: "#report", label: "The report & JSONata fix" },
      { href: "#using", label: "In the Lab & CI" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Other references",
    items: [
      { href: "/start", label: "Getting started" },
      { href: "/lint", label: "Linting & migration" },
      { href: "/fullbars", label: "FullBars" },
      { href: "/transform", label: "Data shaping" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=fullbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];
---

<Reference
  title="Truthiness portability"
  subtitle="The same template can take a different branch on a different engine: false, null, 0, '', [], and {} are not all falsy everywhere. Analyse mode renders against your real data and flags every condition that would flip — with a rule-free fix — so a template stays portable."
  groups={groups}
  dialect="fullbars"
>
  <style>
    .ambig { display: inline-flex; gap: 0.35rem; flex-wrap: wrap; font: 600 12px/1.6 var(--font-mono); margin: 0.4rem 0 0; }
    .ambig code { padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-strong); border-radius: 5px;
      background: var(--bg-1); color: var(--fg-secondary); }
  </style>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>Why truthiness travels badly</h2>
    <p>
      A FlatBars template is logic-light, but it still asks yes/no questions:
      <code>{t("{{#if bio}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{#unless items}}")}</code>. The answer
      depends on a <b>truthiness rule</b>, and engines disagree. FullBars uses
      Handlebars's rule; Mustache, StringTemplate, and others differ. Six values are
      the flashpoints — the <b>ambiguous set</b>:
    </p>
    <div class="ambig">
      <code>false</code><code>null</code><code>0</code><code>""</code><code>[]</code><code>{t("{}")}</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>false</code> and <code>null</code> are falsy everywhere — portable. The other four are
      not: <code>0</code> is falsy in Handlebars but truthy in the
      <b>language-agnostic Mustache</b> rule (the Ruby/Python implementations);
      <code>""</code> the same; <code>[]</code> varies; <code>{t("{}")}</code> is truthy almost
      everywhere but falsy under the <code>presence</code> rule. Test one of these in a
      condition and your template can render a different branch on someone else's engine.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
      One sharp edge worth naming: <b><code>mustache.js</code></b>, the dominant JavaScript
      Mustache, does <em>not</em> follow that spec rule — it skips a section on
      <code>!value</code>, so <code>0</code> and <code>""</code> are falsy there, exactly as in
      Handlebars. So the report labels the spec reading <code>mustache-spec</code> and its legend
      notes that the engine's <code>handlebars</code> rule is also <code>mustache.js</code>' — a
      <code>mustache-spec</code> flip does <em>not</em> apply to a <code>mustache.js</code> target.
      (FlatBars' own MinBars defaults to <code>mustache-spec</code>; render with
      <code>flatbars --mustache-js</code> or <code>renderMinbarsCompat</code>
      for byte-for-byte <code>mustache.js</code> truthiness.)
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Analyse mode</b> finds these without guessing. It <em>renders your template
      against your data</em> through the real engine — byte-identical output, no second
      interpreter — and records every truthiness decision, then replays it against the
      other named rules. A condition whose branch flips is a <b>portability finding</b>,
      reported with a located, rule-free fix. "No findings" on this data is a proof the
      render is portable <em>for that data</em> — not a guarantee for all data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Empty string ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="empty-string">
    <h2>Empty string</h2>
    <p>
      The user left the field blank, so the value is <code>""</code>. Handlebars treats
      that as falsy and shows the <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code> branch; spec Mustache treats it
      as truthy and shows the filled branch — same template, two outputs. (<code>mustache.js</code>
      sides with Handlebars here.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse engine="fullbars" template={ex.emptyString.template} data={ex.emptyString.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The <b>flatbars analyse</b> pane runs live: the finding is the engine's, not hand-typed —
      edit <code>bio</code> to a non-empty string and it clears. The fix it names
      (<code>{t("(ne bio \"\")")}</code>) makes the decision rule-free.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── Zero ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="zero">
    <h2>Zero</h2>
    <p>
      <code>0</code> is the canonical divergence: falsy in Handlebars (and
      <code>mustache.js</code>), truthy under spec Mustache and the minimal/presence rules.
      "0 unread" silently becomes "all caught up" on the wrong engine.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse engine="fullbars" template={ex.zero.template} data={ex.zero.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      <code>includeZero=true</code> keeps <code>0</code> truthy on Handlebars <em>only</em> (not
      portable); the rule-free fix the pane names — <code>{t("(ne count 0)")}</code> — holds on
      every engine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Empty array & object ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="collections">
    <h2>Empty array &amp; empty object</h2>
    <p>
      An empty <b>array</b> is falsy in Handlebars and Mustache — but the portable spelling
      is still to <em>iterate</em>: <code>{t("{{#each}}…{{else}}…{{/each}}")}</code> renders
      the empty branch the same way on every engine, without leaning on `if`-falsiness.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse engine="fullbars" template={ex.emptyArray.template} data={ex.emptyArray.data} />
    <p>
      An empty <b>object</b> is the non-obvious one: truthy in Handlebars,
      <code>mustache.js</code>, <em>and</em> spec Mustache, so it looks safe — but it flips under
      the <code>presence</code> rule. The analyser still catches it (watch the pane).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse engine="fullbars" template={ex.emptyObject.template} data={ex.emptyObject.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── What's portable ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="portable">
    <h2>What's already portable</h2>
    <p>
      Not every condition is a problem. <code>false</code> (and <code>null</code>) are falsy
      on every engine, so a condition testing them is safe — the analyser confirms it with a
      <code>✓</code> line, positive evidence rather than silence.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse engine="fullbars" template={ex.portable.template} data={ex.portable.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The pane says it plainly — <em>every condition agrees across engines for this data</em>.
      <code>false</code> (and <code>null</code>) are falsy everywhere, so there's nothing to fix.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── Observed vs potential ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="potential">
    <h2>Observed vs potential</h2>
    <p>
      Everything above is an <b>observed</b> finding — your data <em>actually</em> held the
      ambiguous value, so the analyser saw the divergence on this render. But a green run
      only proves the template is portable <em>for that sample</em>: <code>{t("{{#if count}}")}</code>
      on <code>count: 5</code> says nothing about <code>count: 0</code>. Hand-crafting
      adversarial data for every path does not scale.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the analyser also reasons <b>symbolically</b>. For every condition whose observed
      value is <em>not</em> ambiguous, it derives the <b>same-type</b> ambiguous value the path
      could hold — a number could be <code>0</code>, a non-empty string could be <code>""</code>,
      a list <code>[]</code>, an object <code>{t("{}")}</code> — and reports it as a
      <b>potential</b> finding. This is a pure post-process over the render: its output is a
      function of the <em>template</em>, not the sample, so it is stable enough to gate in CI.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse engine="fullbars" template={ex.potential.template} data={ex.potential.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The pane reads <b>0 observed · 1 potential</b>: <code>count</code> is <code>5</code> here
      (portable now), but it's a number, so the analyser flags that it <em>would diverge if it
      held</em> <code>0</code>. Bump <code>count</code> to <code>0</code> in the data and the
      same finding turns <b>observed</b>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── Suppressing with a schema ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="suppress">
    <h2>Suppressing with a PathSchema</h2>
    <p>
      Potential and miss findings are <em>what-ifs</em> — sometimes the host knows they can't
      happen: "<code>count</code> is always a present positive number". Rather than bake a type
      system into the engine, analyse takes a <b>PathSchema</b> — a host callback
      <code>(path, value) =&gt; boolean</code> ("could this path hold this value?") — and drops the
      what-ifs it rules out. It is the third pluggable seam, alongside truthiness and i18n; the
      default admits everything, so it costs nothing unused.
    </p>
    <p>
      The card below has both a <b>potential</b> (<code>count</code> could be <code>0</code>) and a
      <b>miss</b> (<code>user.naem</code> is absent from a present <code>user</code> — a typo). Type
      a path into <code>analyse.pathSchema</code> — try <code>count</code>, then
      <code>count, user.naem</code> — and watch the findings vanish, with a live
      <b>N&nbsp;suppressed</b> count. That is <code>analyzeWith</code>, the seam the Lab's
      <code>config.yaml</code> exposes.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible analyse pathSchemaDemo engine="fullbars" template={ex.suppress.template} data={ex.suppress.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      Suppression is advisory: it never changes what renders, only which what-ifs the report
      shows. Observed findings — the ones your data actually hit — are <em>never</em> suppressed,
      because they already happened.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── Portability at scale ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="gallery">
    <h2>Portability at scale</h2>
    <p>
      The cards above isolate one ambiguous value each. Real templates mix them. Here the
      analyser runs live over a handful of production-shaped templates — each tile shows its
      finding profile (the same <code>analyze</code> the Lab and CI use), and <b>Lab ↗</b> opens
      it straight into the Truthiness panel. The spread is the point: an observed divergence, a
      latent potential, a typo'd miss, and a fully portable case can all hide in everyday markup.
    </p>
    <AnalyseGallery client:visible items={gallery} />
    <p class="tip">
      These are <b>FullBars</b> templates: analyse is the FullBars engine, and the Mustache
      conformance corpus doesn't exercise truthiness — so a portability gallery is a curated set,
      not a fixture dump. Point <code>flatbars analyse</code> at your own template directory in CI
      to get this profile across your real corpus.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── The report & fix ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="report">
    <h2>The report &amp; the JSONata fix</h2>
    <p>
      Each finding is a markdown section: the located tag, the value it tested, the rules
      that flip, and a concrete fix that names only helpers that exist
      (<code>ne</code>/<code>eq</code>/<code>each</code>). The fixes above are <em>in-template</em>.
      When the cleaner fix is in the <b>data</b>, <code>--emit-jsonata</code> generates a
      reviewable <a href="/transform">JSONata</a> scaffold that normalises each offending
      bare path out of the ambiguous zone <em>before</em> the data reaches any engine —
      e.g. mapping a blank <code>bio</code> to <code>null</code>. It is a scaffold to review,
      never applied automatically: what "normalise" means is your call.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────── Using it ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="using">
    <h2>In the Lab &amp; in CI</h2>
    <p>
      <b>In the Lab</b> — <b>Open in Lab</b> on any example above carries the template and
      data over; switch to the <b>Truthiness</b> panel beside the output to see the findings
      update live as you edit. The <code>?vendored=</code> corpus turns the Lab into a
      portability gallery. To watch <b>suppression</b> interactively, edit
      <code>config.yaml</code> → <code>analyse.pathSchema</code>: add a path you guarantee
      safe (e.g. <code>[count]</code>) and its potential/miss findings vanish from the panel,
      with a note naming the active schema — the <code>PathSchema</code> seam, live.
    </p>
    <p><b>In CI / a script</b> — the same report on the command line:</p>
    <pre class="cli"><code>$ flatbars analyse template.hbs data.json
$ flatbars analyse template.hbs data.json --emit-jsonata   # the cleanup scaffold</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
      The CLI and the Lab share one <code>reportMarkdown</code> through the
      <code>flatbars-js</code> bundle, so they can never disagree — and this page's examples
      are run through that same <code>analyze</code> in CI, so the findings shown here are
      exactly what the engine produces.
    </p>
  </section>
</Reference>
